The user wants a program to find common elements in two arrays without extra data structures (no sets, hash maps). So use nested loops, print common elements. Handle duplicates? Typically we print each common element once — but avoiding extra structures means we can check by scanning previously printed elements in array itself... We can avoid duplicates by checking if the element already appeared earlier in arr1 (scan left part of arr1). That uses no extra data structure.

Provide code in C (or Java/Python). Let me give a clear explanation and code, maybe C and Python. Complexity O(n*m) or O(n²).

Write solution:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

void findCommon(int a[], int b[], int n, int m) {
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        // skip duplicates in a
        int j;
        for (j = 0; j < i; j++)
            if (a[j] == a[i]) break;
        if (j < i) continue;
        // check in b
        for (int k = 0; k < m; k++)
            if (a[i] == b[k]) { printf("%d ", a[i]); break; }
    }
}
```

